#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:381
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid "What changed in August 2026"
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:382
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"Before this work, I had done a lot of manual reverse engineering. I would inspect a "
"function until I had an explanation, then test that explanation against the program. "
"Getting further meant spending more of my own time on the next function. It also meant"
" keeping an increasingly large picture in my head."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:389
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"Most of my RE work is on games. I reconstruct them so their behavior can be understood"
" and eventually carried into a port or a mod. That is the experience behind this "
"article. The method can be useful elsewhere, but each field needs to establish what it"
" can check against a reliable reference."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:396
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"In August 2026, I started exploring agent-driven RE seriously. I wanted to see how far"
" an agent could get with access to the program and ordinary development tools. Touhou "
"reconstruction gave me a substantial project in which to find out."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:402
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"The early progress was startling. An investigation could keep moving without me "
"choosing every individual step. A failed comparison might send the agent to another "
"caller. It could write a small diagnostic and use the result to decide what to try "
"next. Giving it that freedom made a difference: I could spend more attention on the "
"project's direction and on whether its checks were trustworthy."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:411
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"The pace got my attention first. Then I began wondering what would survive beyond the "
"current project. Would the next game benefit from everything we had just learned?"
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:418
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid "TH08: building on human work"
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <a href="https://github.com/N0zoM1z0/th08">
#. {ph1}: </a>
#. {ph2}: <a href="https://github.com/GensokyoClub/th08" >
#. {ph3}: </a >
#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:419
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"{ph0}TH08{ph1}, Imperishable Night, began as a continuation of {ph2}GensokyoClub's "
"reconstruction{ph3}. Their public source gave me a substantial foundation. It came "
"with knowledge of the build and a history of contributions that I preserved in the "
"continuation."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:428
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"The imported history ends at the public 10 August checkpoint. My independent "
"continuation began on 13 August. By 19 August, the ledger recorded source for all "
"1,107 identified game functions."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:433
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"A playable Linux reconstruction port was committed on 24 August, roughly eleven days "
"after the continuation began. A Web edition followed. The native Linux 64-bit release "
"arrived on 30 August."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:438
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"What struck me was that the work had reached a program people could run. Getting there"
" required following problems beyond an individual function. Even when two functions "
"looked correct in isolation, they could accidentally use separate copies of state that"
" should have been shared."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <strong>
#. {ph1}: </strong>
#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:445
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"This made reference checks central to the work. I call them {ph0}oracles{ph1}. A code "
"comparison can tell us whether a reconstructed function reproduces the original "
"instructions. A runtime check can tell us whether an exercised route reaches the "
"expected state. The agent proposes an explanation and tests it; a mismatch gives it "
"something specific to investigate."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:453
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid "An exact match with the wrong constant"
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:454
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"An oracle is software someone wrote. TH08 gave me a clear reminder of how much can "
"depend on that software."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <code>0.0</code>
#. {ph1}: <code>128.0</code>
#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:458
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"In September, a bug reported by a downstream Switch port led back to item auto-"
"collection. The reconstructed source checked player power against {ph0}. The original "
"used {ph1}, the maximum-power threshold."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:464
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"Normal power is non-negative, so the reconstructed power check was effectively always "
"satisfied. Above the collection line, the game could attract items without requiring "
"maximum power. The other exceptions in the condition were correct. This one constant "
"changed the behavior."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <strong >
#. {ph1}: </strong >
#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:471
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid "{ph0}Yet the function had already passed the exact comparison.{ph1}"
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:477
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"A rebuild can put a constant at a different address. The comparison tool accounted for"
" that by adjusting addresses in the compiled instructions before comparing them with "
"the original. But it never checked the floating-point value stored at the referenced "
"address."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <code>128.0</code>
#. {ph1}: <code>0.0</code>
#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:484
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"That left a hole in the check. It could point the reconstructed instruction at the "
"original's {ph0} while the source still said {ph1}. The instruction bytes matched. The"
" source meant something different."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <a href="https://github.com/N0zoM1z0/th08/pull/11" >
#. {ph1}: </a >
#. {ph2}: <a href="https://github.com/N0zoM1z0/th08/pull/13">
#. {ph3}: </a>
#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:490
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"The {ph0}2 September fix{ph1} corrected the source and made the comparison check the "
"referenced constant's actual bytes. A {ph2}wider audit{ph3} then examined 1,548 "
"floating-literal references. It found another twelve incorrect references across five "
"accepted functions."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:501
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"The comparison now checks every such floating literal. Its tests include deliberately "
"wrong values to make sure they are rejected. We also had to revisit results that had "
"passed the weaker check."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <strong>
#. {ph1}: </strong>
#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:506
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"{ph0}The oracle needed reconstructing too.{ph1} Repairing it was part of repairing the"
" game."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:510
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"That matters when the team is mostly one person working with agents. I cannot "
"personally inspect every line they produce, so much of my confidence rests on their "
"checks. A shared oracle's blind spot can affect many investigations before I notice "
"it. I have to understand what the checker actually verifies and test it against cases "
"that ought to fail."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <strong >
#. {ph1}: </strong >
#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:518
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"As the work continued, those checks became part of the repository. So did the reasons "
"for source changes and notes that let a later session pick up where an earlier one "
"stopped. {ph0}The repository was becoming the project's working memory.{ph1}"
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <strong >
#. {ph1}: </strong >
#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:527
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"{ph0}A successful batch gave us both recovered code and a better environment for the "
"next batch.{ph1}"
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:535
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid "Two different ideas of reconstruction"
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <a
#. href="https://web.archive.org/web/20260909051514/https://github.com/GensokyoClub/th08#important-
#. notice" >
#. {ph1}: </a >
#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:536
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"GensokyoClub's {ph0}public README{ph1} makes its disagreement with this kind of work "
"explicit. The notice announces that further development will happen privately until "
"completion. One passage reads:"
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:547
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"The rise of grifters (AI decompilations and ports) in this space taking from our work "
"paints a bad image for future decompilation efforts…"
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:553
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"The notice also describes the psychological toll on the maintainers. Their "
"contribution policy excludes pull requests produced primarily with AI. They have put "
"their free time into difficult work, and that work helped make my continuation "
"possible. I respect the effort behind it. The disagreement I want to discuss is about "
"how reconstruction should proceed and how a contribution should be judged."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:562
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"In the manual workflow I knew, developing an understanding of a function and "
"reconstructing it usually fell to the same person. A project relied heavily on that "
"person's expertise. Trust in the contributor mattered because so much of the reasoning"
" happened while they worked."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:569
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"Existing projects already preserve knowledge in their source and build tools. What "
"changed for me was that an agent could use that knowledge to pursue the next "
"investigation on its own."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:574
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"In my continuation, I decide the objective and the standard for accepting a result. "
"The agent has broad freedom to investigate. Its proposed reconstruction then has to "
"survive the relevant checks. I want another person to be able to examine why we chose "
"an implementation, even if an agent did most of the work."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:581
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"This can be a difficult transition. Years of careful work may become the foundation "
"for a continuation that moves much faster. That raises real questions about credit. It"
" also changes what maintainers need to know before accepting a contribution."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:587
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"The industrial analogy helps me think about this. In a craft, much of the process "
"depends on the skill of the person carrying it out. Machinery changes where that skill"
" is needed. Someone still has to design the process and recognize when its output is "
"wrong. Different communities can choose how much of that change they want to take on."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:595
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"My choice is to continue openly, with the inherited work credited and its history "
"preserved. I want the new work to be reviewable. That gives us a way to ask how far "
"this approach can go and to learn from what goes wrong along the way."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <a
#. href="https://github.com/GensokyoClub/th08/blob/e874b98e210b3be1ed21c5e03d54b883eac5069c/README.md#important-
#. notice" >
#. {ph1}: </a >
#. {ph2}: <a
#. href="https://github.com/GensokyoClub/th08/blob/f345c7dee07b849f2ff93a0029ab5ede126bf12c/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md"
#. >
#. {ph3}: </a >
#. {ph4}: <a
#. href="https://github.com/N0zoM1z0/th08/blob/d2a00f4acb4a13916e07a00bd13f714961714628/README.md#credits-
#. and-provenance" >
#. {ph5}: </a >
#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:601
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"Source: GensokyoClub's {ph0}README notice{ph1}, checked on 10 October 2026, and its "
"{ph2}contribution policy{ph3}. The quotation is a shortened excerpt. TH08's "
"{ph4}credits and provenance{ph5} record the continuation boundary."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:619
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid "TH095: experience starts compounding"
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <a href="https://github.com/N0zoM1z0/th095">
#. {ph1}: </a>
#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:620
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"{ph0}TH095{ph1}, Shoot the Bullet, made the value of that experience much easier to "
"see. Its repository began on 29 August with zero confirmed game functions in the "
"ledger. We still had to learn the game. But we already knew much more about how to "
"begin a reconstruction and how to keep it moving."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:628
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"By 7 September, all 697 identified game functions had source. By 8 September, 696 had "
"been accepted as exact comparisons. The whole program linked on 9 September. The "
"Windows i386 reconstruction was marked playable on 10 September, about twelve days "
"after initialization."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:635
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"I found this more exciting than the first project's speed. A fresh target could "
"benefit from work done on another game. The experience was already present in the "
"tools and in the way the project was organized."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:641
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"For example, TH08 had taught us to pay attention to the assembled program early. If "
"several recovered functions depend on the same state, their isolated comparisons leave"
" an important question open. We need to see them working together. That lesson helped "
"shape how we approached TH095's whole-program build."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:648
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"A lesson that stays in my head is useful while I am there. Once it becomes a check "
"that another session can run, it can keep helping after I have moved on. The next "
"agent can use the result without repeating the investigation that led to it."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:654
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"The floating-literal bug belongs in that memory too. It explains why checking a "
"reference also requires checking the data behind it. Keeping that correction with the "
"code helps later projects avoid inheriting the old check's blind spot."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <strong >
#. {ph1}: </strong >
#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:660
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"{ph0}The method becomes part of the starting material for the next game.{ph1} We can "
"spend more of the next project's effort on what is actually new about its target."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:668
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"The same benefit is available to people joining later. They can inspect a decision and"
" rerun its check before continuing the work. They do not have to reconstruct the "
"entire project's history to find out why the source looks the way it does."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:676
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid "TH04: the workflow survives a different architecture"
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <a href="https://github.com/N0zoM1z0/th04">
#. {ph1}: </a>
#. {ph2}: <a href="https://github.com/nmlgc/ReC98">
#. {ph3}: </a>
#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:677
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"{ph0}TH04{ph1}, Lotus Land Story, took this work into the PC-98 DOS era. The target "
"was now a 16-bit environment with four cooperating programs. Understanding its "
"hardware behavior required different evidence from the Windows games. Existing "
"{ph2}ReC98 work{ph3} gave us valuable knowledge and source material here too."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:686
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"The DOS reconstruction is now working. In my manual testing, I played complete Normal "
"routes through their endings and checked saves. The current work is a native 64-bit "
"port. Establishing a working DOS version first gives that port a reference."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:692
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"The architecture changed what we needed to investigate. It also changed the compiler "
"and runtime against which we checked our work. But the agent could still follow a "
"question through to a result and let that result guide the next experiment."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:698
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"Consider the transition from gameplay to an ending. We need to know what state carries"
" across that boundary and which program is responsible for it. That is something we "
"can investigate against the DOS product. Once the evidence and checks are available, "
"an agent can work through the question much as it did on a Windows title."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:706
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"This is why TH04 matters to the argument. A substantial platform change did not force "
"us to start over with a new way of working. The architecture defined the problem; the "
"workflow still gave us a way to solve it."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:712
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"For the 64-bit port, we can now examine the new implementation against behavior "
"already recovered on DOS. The knowledge from reconstruction gives the port something "
"to build on."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <a
#. href="https://github.com/N0zoM1z0/th04/blob/bb9faca54e2eb3c0d6c757c75d59ac29d2cd5e93/docs/RE_HANDOFF.md"
#. >
#. {ph1}: </a >
#. {ph2}: <a href="https://github.com/N0zoM1z0/th04/tree/port/modern-64" >
#. {ph3}: </a >
#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:717
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"Project state as of 10 October 2026: {ph0}DOS reconstruction and manual testing{ph1} ·"
" {ph2}64-bit port{ph3}. The port remains in development."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:730
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid "From exact code to readable code"
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:731
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid "Once a reconstruction works, I want someone else to be able to understand it."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:735
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"To me, assembly and raw offsets can feel like old friends. I realise this is a "
"slightly unusual definition of “readable.” Most people would prefer to follow the "
"game's logic without keeping the executable's memory layout in their head."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <strong>
#. {ph1}: </strong>
#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:741
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"That is where {ph0}semantic reconstruction{ph1} comes in. A recovered field may still "
"be known mainly by its offset. We follow how the game uses it until we can explain its"
" role. Then we can give it a meaningful name and a type that fits the evidence. We "
"keep the reasoning with the source so the next person can see where that "
"interpretation came from."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:749
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"This becomes especially important for a port. An absolute address tells me where "
"something lived in the old executable. It gives a 64-bit implementation little help in"
" deciding which object should own that state. To move the behavior safely, we need to "
"recover the relationship behind the old memory access."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:756
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid "The order I now use is:"
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <strong>
#. {ph1}: </strong>
#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:758
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"{ph0}Recover an exact baseline.{ph1} Compile the reconstructed pieces with the "
"historical compiler. Compare the relevant code and data with the original executable. "
"Record unresolved differences so the next phase has a clear starting point."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <strong>
#. {ph1}: </strong>
#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:765
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"{ph0}Build and play it on the original platform.{ph1} Link those pieces into the real "
"program using the original architecture and compiler. Exercise important gameplay "
"paths. This is where we can find problems with shared state or initialization that an "
"isolated function comparison missed."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <strong >
#. {ph1}: </strong >
#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:772
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"{ph0}Reconstruct the semantics against both references.{ph1} Take one coherent part of"
" the game at a time and establish what its recovered source means. Improve its "
"representation while preserving the exact comparisons and the playable historical "
"build."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <strong>
#. {ph1}: </strong>
#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:781
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"{ph0}Make the modern port.{ph1} Move the established behavior into the new "
"environment, such as a native 64-bit build. The reconstructed original-platform game "
"remains a reference for comparing how the port behaves."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <strong>
#. {ph1}: </strong>
#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:788
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"The playable build from the second stage becomes a {ph0}second oracle{ph1} during the "
"third. The first oracle checks whether our changed source still reproduces the "
"relevant original code and data. The second checks that the reconstructed program "
"still builds and behaves correctly along the paths we exercise."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:796
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"They catch different mistakes. A type change can alter the generated instructions. An "
"ownership change can leave two parts of the game using different copies of state. "
"Keeping both checks available gives the agent a concrete failure to investigate before"
" carrying a refactor further."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:803
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"A name needs evidence of its own. An exact comparison cannot tell us whether a field "
"really means “invulnerability time.” We have to establish that from how the game "
"writes and uses it. If the meaning remains uncertain, a neutral name is more helpful "
"to the next reader than a confident guess."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <a href="https://github.com/N0zoM1z0/touhou-reconstruction-
#. factory/blob/717e1b8560783ed17a39fdf159d30aa16bb307a7/docs/semantic-
#. reconstruction.md" >
#. {ph1}: </a >
#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:810
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"We learned this order through the projects. TH08 already had playable ports before "
"some of its later historical-platform audits. That made certain defects harder to see."
" The {ph0}Factory's current workflow{ph1} puts the original-platform build first, so "
"semantic work can use it as a reference before porting begins."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <strong >
#. {ph1}: </strong >
#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:821
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"{ph0}Exact reconstruction gives us a reference. Semantic reconstruction makes the "
"recovered knowledge usable.{ph1} A port can then build on both."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:830
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid "What makes this an industrial shift"
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:831
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"These projects changed where I spent my attention. Once agents could carry much of an "
"investigation forward, improving their working environment became one of the most "
"useful things I could do. A better tool could help with every later function that "
"needed it."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:838
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"Autonomy matters here. The useful next step often becomes clear only after a failed "
"experiment. An agent needs enough freedom to follow that result somewhere unexpected. "
"If it has to wait for me to prescribe each step, much of the work remains tied to my "
"attention."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:845
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"I expect the agent to make wrong hypotheses. What matters is whether we can test them "
"and learn from the result. A failed check should help it understand the mistake well "
"enough to try again. I still have to decide whether the accumulated evidence supports "
"a project milestone."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:852
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"REA gives the agent access to analysis tools. A question about a function's caller can"
" lead straight into inspecting that caller. The reconstruction project supplies the "
"compiler and its own reference checks. The agent can use those to test the source it "
"proposes and see where its explanation holds up."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:859
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"The TH08 bug shows why those checks deserve engineering attention of their own. When "
"the same comparison is used across hundreds of functions, a gap in it can spread much "
"further than a mistake in one implementation. Testing the checker improves the "
"feedback available to all that later work."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <a href="https://collection.sciencemuseumgroup.org.uk/objects/co51439/watts-
#. steam-engine-indicator" >
#. {ph1}: </a >
#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:866
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"The industrial analogy has a useful historical example here. Boulton and Watt "
"introduced a {ph0}steam-engine indicator in 1796{ph1} to help adjust engine valves. A "
"recording version traced pressure through the piston stroke. It made the engine's "
"internal behavior available for inspection. Our comparison tools serve a similar "
"purpose: they let us examine what the machinery is doing while we improve it."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <strong >
#. {ph1}: </strong >
#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:879
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"{ph0}We are in the early stages of this industrial shift.{ph1} Much of the "
"infrastructure is still immature. Agents can move faster than our checks were designed"
" to support, so the process has to develop alongside them. When we find a defect in a "
"shared tool, we have to repair it and revisit the results it affected. The next "
"project can then inherit a stronger tool."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:889
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"There is also a practical limit to any one conversation. It will end before a large "
"reconstruction is finished. The repository has to make it possible for another session"
" to continue without losing the reason for the last decision."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <a href="https://github.com/N0zoM1z0/touhou-reconstruction-factory" >
#. {ph1}: </a >
#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:895
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"The {ph0}Touhou Reconstruction Factory{ph1} grew out of that need. It gives projects a"
" shared way to carry their checks and lessons forward. Work on one game can improve "
"the starting conditions for another."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:905
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"That is what makes the industrial analogy meaningful to me. Experience starts becoming"
" part of tools that others can use. Improving those tools changes how much the next "
"person—or the next agent—can get done."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:913
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid "The projects we can now consider"
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:914
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"The pace matters because it changes the decision to begin. A game could be fascinating"
" to reverse engineer and still demand more of my own attention than I could "
"realistically give it. Many projects would stay ideas."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:920
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"Now I can see a way to keep such a project moving through repeated investigations. "
"Getting a working reconstruction makes a port more practical. Recovering readable "
"semantics makes it easier for someone else to explore a mod. The effort put into "
"understanding the game can keep paying off after the first version runs."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <strong >
#. {ph1}: </strong >
#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:927
#, python-brace-format
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"I now look at an unfamiliar program and ask: {ph0}what access, feedback and "
"accumulated knowledge would let an agent work on this reliably?{ph1}"
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:934
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"That question makes me consider projects I would previously have left alone. Each one "
"can improve the way we approach the next. I want to keep exploring how far that can "
"take us."
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:942
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid "Project milestones and sources"
msgstr ""

#: website/public/blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html:943
msgctxt "page:blog/touhou-reconstruction/index.html"
msgid ""
"The dates describe recorded project checkpoints, checked against public GitHub history"
" on 10 October 2026. Elapsed time is calendar time between commits. Source presence, "
"exact comparisons, a build and a runtime result each name a different milestone."
msgstr ""

#. {ph0}: <strong>
#. {ph1}: </strong>
#. {ph2}: <a
#. href="https://github.com/N0zoM1z0/th08/commit/001bf3e9c91cc35b79c7a0e36b3565b86f494362"
#. >
